1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,323
Why does Greenland look as big as

2
00:00:02,323 --> 00:00:04,224
Africa on almost every map?

3
00:00:04,574 --> 00:00:06,732
On the map you probably grew up

4
00:00:06,732 --> 00:00:09,656
with, Greenland looks huge. About the same

5
00:00:09,656 --> 00:00:11,187
size as all of Africa.

6
00:00:11,537 --> 00:00:14,426
But in reality, Africa is about fourteen

7
00:00:14,426 --> 00:00:17,893
times bigger. Africa covers about thirty million

8
00:00:17,893 --> 00:00:21,649
square kilometers. Greenland? Just over two million.

9
00:00:21,999 --> 00:00:24,136
So why the lie? Because the Earth

10
00:00:24,136 --> 00:00:25,625
is a ball, and a map is

11
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:28,085
flat. You can't flatten a ball without

12
00:00:28,085 --> 00:00:29,445
stretching something.

13
00:00:29,795 --> 00:00:33,382
The most popular map, the Mercator projection,

14
00:00:33,382 --> 00:00:35,877
was made in 1569 for sailors. It

15
00:00:35,877 --> 00:00:39,542
keeps directions correct, which was perfect for

16
00:00:39,542 --> 00:00:42,817
navigation. But it stretches land more and

17
00:00:42,817 --> 00:00:45,156
more the closer you get to the

18
00:00:45,156 --> 00:00:45,624
poles.

19
00:00:45,974 --> 00:00:48,867
That's why Greenland, Canada and Russia look

20
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:52,023
gigantic, while countries near the equator, like

21
00:00:52,023 --> 00:00:54,522
most of Africa, look much smaller than

22
00:00:54,522 --> 00:00:55,574
they really are.

23
00:00:55,924 --> 00:00:58,309
In fact, you could fit the United

24
00:00:58,309 --> 00:01:01,128
States, China, India and most of Europe

25
00:01:01,128 --> 00:01:03,947
inside Africa, and still have room left

26
00:01:03,947 --> 00:01:04,308
over.

27
00:01:04,658 --> 00:01:06,873
Africa is so big it has fifty

28
00:01:06,873 --> 00:01:10,081
four countries. The Sahara Desert alone is

29
00:01:10,081 --> 00:01:12,678
almost as big as the entire United

30
00:01:12,678 --> 00:01:13,213
States.

31
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:15,756
Look at Alaska. On a normal map

32
00:01:15,756 --> 00:01:17,807
it looks almost as big as the

33
00:01:17,807 --> 00:01:20,496
rest of the United States. In reality,

34
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:23,255
the lower forty eight states are almost

35
00:01:23,255 --> 00:01:24,528
five times bigger.

36
00:01:24,878 --> 00:01:27,638
And Antarctica looks like an endless white

37
00:01:27,638 --> 00:01:29,937
strip across the bottom of the map.

38
00:01:29,937 --> 00:01:33,223
That's just the Mercator projection stretching the

39
00:01:33,223 --> 00:01:34,734
South Pole into a line.

40
00:01:35,084 --> 00:01:38,344
Mapmakers have tried other projections, like the

41
00:01:38,344 --> 00:01:41,535
Gall-Peters map, which shows countries at their

42
00:01:41,535 --> 00:01:44,252
true size. But then shapes look squashed

43
00:01:44,252 --> 00:01:46,696
and stretched. Every flat map has to

44
00:01:46,696 --> 00:01:48,055
sacrifice something.

45
00:01:48,405 --> 00:01:50,984
The only map that gets everything right

46
00:01:50,984 --> 00:01:53,166
is a globe. That's why pilots and

47
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:56,076
ships planning long routes use great circles

48
00:01:56,076 --> 00:01:58,259
on a globe, not straight lines on

49
00:01:58,259 --> 00:01:58,986
a flat map.

50
00:01:59,336 --> 00:02:01,197
So next time you see a world

51
00:02:01,197 --> 00:02:03,456
map, remember: the size you see is

52
00:02:03,456 --> 00:02:06,047
not the size that's real. Which country

53
00:02:06,047 --> 00:02:08,307
surprised you the most? Tell me in

54
00:02:08,307 --> 00:02:09,171
the comments.

